    "markdown": "# Circular Linked Lists: Endless Loops & Ring Schedulers\n\nWhile linear lists terminate with a null address reference, circular linked lists wrap back. The final node points directly to the head node, forming an endless traversal ring.\n\nThis chapter explores **Circular Linked Lists**. We focus on loop invariants, traversal termination boundaries, and operating system round-robin schedulers.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Loop Invariants: Eliminating Null References\n\nIn a circular list, there are no `null` pointers. If you start a traversal loop, it will run forever unless you detect the starting point.\n\n```cpp\n// Traverse a circular list starting at head\nListNode* curr = head;\nif (head != nullptr) {\n    do {\n        // Process curr node\n        curr = curr->next;\n    } while (curr != head); // Loop termination invariant\n}\n```\n\n### Circular List Wrapping Linkage\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#f472b6\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Circular Linked Ring Layout</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Nodes */}\n    <circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"50\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"40\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Head</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 54,50 L 106,50\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-circ)\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"50\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">N1</text>\n\n    <path d=\"M 134,50 L 186,50\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-circ)\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"50\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"200\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Tail</text>\n\n    {/* Return Loop path */}\n    <path d=\"M 200,36 C 200,0 40,0 40,36\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#eab308\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 1\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-circ-yellow)\"/>\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"10\" fill=\"#eab308\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Next wraps to Head</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-circ\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#f472b6\" />\n    </marker>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-circ-yellow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#eab308\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Real-World Systems: Round-Robin Scheduling\n\nOperating systems run multiple threads using CPU scheduling algorithms. The simplest algorithm is **Round-Robin Scheduling**.\n\nThe OS scheduler places runnable process threads in a circular linked list queue. The CPU executes each process for a fixed time threshold (quantum):\n- If the thread finishes, it is removed from the loop.\n- If the quantum expires, the scheduler shifts CPU registers to the next node in the circular list (`curr = curr->next`), automatically placing the previous process back at the end of the execution cycle.\n\n### Round-Robin Scheduler Ring\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Round-Robin Process Scheduler Ring</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(60, 60)\">\n    {/* Circular nodes */}\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Proc A</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"50\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"120\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Active</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"85\" cy=\"110\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"85\" y=\"113\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Proc C</text>\n\n    {/* Arcs */}\n    <path d=\"M 70,50 A 20,20 0 0,1 100,50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rr)\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 120,70 A 20,20 0 0,1 105,110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rr)\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 65,110 A 20,20 0 0,1 50,70\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-rr)\"/>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-rr\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Linked List Cycle (LeetCode 141)](https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle/): Loop pointer tracking.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Master cycle detection mechanics.\n* [Circular Queue (LeetCode 622)](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-circular-queue/): Design loop limits.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Connect circular math concepts to queue buffers.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Insert into a Sorted Circular Linked List (LeetCode 708)](https://leetcode.com/problems/insert-into-a-sorted-circular-linked-list/): Value insertion bounds.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices searching transition nodes in endless lists.\n* [Linked List Cycle II (LeetCode 142)](https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle-ii/): Loop start offset.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Utilizes tortoise and hare pointer collisions to locate cycles.\n",
