    "slug": "fifo-queues",
    "markdown": "# FIFO Queues: Message Pipelines & Ring Buffers\n\nTo process events sequentially in the exact order they arrive, systems use **FIFO Queues**.\n\nThis chapter explores **FIFO Queues** (First-In, First-Out). We focus on circular index mapping, shifting overheads, and multi-thread task delivery.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Circular Arrays vs. Shifting Indexes\n\nIf you implement a queue using a simple array:\n- **Enqueue**: Write to the index next to the tail ($O(1)$).\n- **Dequeue**: Remove from the front. If you shift all remaining items forward to index 0, the operation costs $O(N)$ copies.\n\nTo maintain $O(1)$ performance, we use a **Circular Ring Buffer**:\n- Maintain `front` and `rear` index pointers.\n- Do not shift elements on dequeue. Instead, increment the `front` index.\n- Use modulo calculations to wrap indices when they reach the end of the array:\n\n$$\\text{index} = (\\text{index} + 1) \\bmod \\text{capacity}$$\n\n### Circular Ring Buffer Index Wrapping\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#f59e0b\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Circular Ring Buffer Layout</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(60, 60)\">\n    {/* Cells in circular ring */}\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">10</text>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"76\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-size=\"7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Idx 0 (F)</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"50\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">20</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"76\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-size=\"7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Idx 1 (R)</text>\n\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#374151\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 1\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"103\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">_</text>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"126\" fill=\"#71717a\" font-size=\"7\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Idx 2</text>\n\n    {/* Arcs */}\n    <path d=\"M 50,32 A 25,25 0 0,1 100,32\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    <path d=\"M 118,50 A 25,25 0 0,1 118,100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#374151\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 1\"/>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n\nThis achieves true $O(1)$ performance for all queue operations.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Implement Queue using Stacks (LeetCode 232)](https://leetcode.com/problems/implement-queue-using-stacks/): Reverse structures.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build queue transformations.\n* [Design Circular Queue (LeetCode 622)](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-circular-queue/): Modulo index math.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Master buffer wrap-around calculations.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Design Circular Deque (LeetCode 641)](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-circular-deque/): Double-ended queue.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Extends circular arrays to support inputs and outputs at both ends.\n* [Rotting Oranges (LeetCode 994)](https://leetcode.com/problems/rotting-oranges/): BFS coordinate queue.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices mapping layers in coordinate networks.\n",
