    "slug": "binomial-heaps",
    "markdown": "# Binomial Heaps: Forests of Power-of-Two Trees & Binary Math\n\nWhile leftist heaps merge in $O(\\log N)$ time, they use arbitrary recursive pointer swings. To structure merges cleanly using binary addition principles, we use a **Binomial Heap**.\n\nThis chapter explores **Binomial Heaps**. We focus on binomial tree definitions, tree-linking properties, and the binary addition analogy for heap merges.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Defining Binomial Trees: $B_k$ Structures\n\nA binomial heap is a collection (forest) of **Binomial Trees** satisfying two rules:\n1. Every binomial tree in the heap obeys the heap-ordered property (parent key is smaller than children keys).\n2. For any order $k$, there is at most one binomial tree of order $k$ in the heap.\n\nA binomial tree of order $k$ (denoted as $B_k$) is defined recursively:\n- $B_0$ consists of a single node.\n- $B_k$ is formed by linking two $B_{k-1}$ trees together, placing one as the leftmost child of the other's root.\n\n$$\\text{Nodes in } B_k = 2^k \\quad \\text{Height of } B_k = k$$\n\n### Binomial Tree Levels\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#f472b6\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Binomial Trees B0, B1, B2</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* B0 */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(10, 20)\">\n      <circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <text x=\"0\" y=\"3\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">B0</text>\n    </g>\n\n    {/* B1 */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(100, 20)\">\n      <circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"0\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <text x=\"0\" y=\"3\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">B1</text>\n      \n      <path d=\"M 0,10 L 0,40\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <circle cx=\"0\" cy=\"50\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n    </g>\n\n    {/* B2 */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(220, 20)\">\n      <circle cx=\"30\" cy=\"0\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <text x=\"30\" y=\"3\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">B2</text>\n\n      <path d=\"M 30,10 L 10,40\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <circle cx=\"10\" cy=\"50\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      \n      <path d=\"M 30,10 L 50,40\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/>\n      <path d=\"M 50,60 L 50,90\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"100\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    </g>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Binomial Heap Merges & Binary Addition\n\nBecause each tree $B_k$ has exactly $2^k$ nodes, the presence or absence of a tree of order $k$ in the heap corresponds directly to the binary bits of the total node count $N$.\n- If $N = 13$, the binary representation is $1101_2$.\n- This means the heap contains exactly three binomial trees: $B_3$ ($8$ nodes), $B_2$ ($4$ nodes), and $B_0$ ($1$ node).\n\nMerging two binomial heaps is modeled on binary addition (with carry-overs):\n- We scan through both forests from order $k = 0$ upward.\n- If we have two trees of order $k$, we link them to form a single tree of order $k+1$ (the \"carry bit\").\n- The links execute in $O(\\log N)$ steps.\n\n### Binary Addition Analogy\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Forest Merge via Binary Addition</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Addition blocks */}\n    <text x=\"0\" y=\"20\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-size=\"10\" font-family=\"monospace\">Heap 1 (13 nodes): 1 1 0 1  (B3, B2, B0)</text>\n    <text x=\"0\" y=\"45\" fill=\"#a1a1aa\" font-size=\"10\" font-family=\"monospace\">Heap 2 (7 nodes):  0 1 1 1  (B2, B1, B0)</text>\n    <line x1=\"0\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n    \n    <text x=\"0\" y=\"75\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"10\" font-family=\"monospace\">Result (20 nodes): 1 0 1 0 0  (B4, B2)</text>\n    <text x=\"0\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#eab308\" font-size=\"8\" font-family=\"monospace\">Carry steps happen when linking two trees of equal order</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Add Binary (LeetCode 67)](https://leetcode.com/problems/add-binary/): Bitwise addition.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build core carry-propagation logic.\n* [Merge Two Sorted Lists (LeetCode 21)](https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-two-sorted-lists/): Merge sorted nodes.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Teaches you basic list merging pointer rules.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Merge k Sorted Lists (LeetCode 23)](https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-k-sorted-lists/): Multi-way merge optimization.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Optimizes sorting lists by merging queues recursively.\n",
