    "slug": "fibonacci-heaps",
    "markdown": "# Fibonacci Heaps: Lazy Consolidation & Constant Amortized Decretions\n\nWhile binary and leftist heaps perform operations in $O(\\log N)$ time, pathfinders (like Dijkstra's shortest path algorithm) perform many more `decrease-key` operations than insertions or extractions. To speed up priority adjustments to $O(1)$ amortized time, we use a **Fibonacci Heap**.\n\nThis chapter explores **Fibonacci Heaps**. We focus on lazy tree structures, cascading node cuts, and consolidation passes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Lazy Evaluation: Postponing Consolidation\n\nInstead of maintaining rigid structure rules during insertion, a Fibonacci heap is \"lazy\":\n- **Insert**: Create a new single-node tree and add it to the heap's root list ($O(1)$ constant time).\n- **Decrease-Key**: Cut the node from its parent and add it to the root list ($O(1)$ amortized).\n- **Extract-Min**: This is where the work is done. Remove the minimum node, promote its children to the root list, and then consolidate the root list by merging trees of equal order until at most one tree of each order remains.\n\n### Cascading Cut Steps\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fibonacci Heap Cascading Cut</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Parent node */}\n    <circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"20\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"100\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">P (20)</text>\n\n    {/* Child node to be cut */}\n    <path d=\"M 90,30 L 60,60\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 1\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"70\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"73\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">C (10)</text>\n\n    {/* Cut arrow and promotion to root list */}\n    <path d=\"M 50,54 Q 50,15 150,15\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-fib-cut)\"/>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"8\" fill=\"#ef4444\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Cut & Promote to Root list</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-fib-cut\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#ef4444\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\nBecause we postpone consolidation, insertions and decrease-key updates execute in $O(1)$ amortized time.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Design Linked List (LeetCode 707)](https://leetcode.com/problems/design-linked-list/): Pointer tree structures.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build tree node allocation skills.\n* [Merge Two Sorted Lists (LeetCode 21)](https://leetcode.com/problems/merge-two-sorted-lists/): Node additions.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Teaches you basic list pointer operations.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Network Delay Time (LeetCode 743)](https://leetcode.com/problems/network-delay-time/): Shortest path searches.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Uses Dijkstra's algorithm to compute shortest paths over grid networks.\n* [Path with Maximum Probability (LeetCode 1514)](https://leetcode.com/problems/path-with-maximum-probability/): Pathfinding updates.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices updating node key limits in priority networks.\n",
