    "slug": "selection-sort",
    "markdown": "# Selection Sort: Sorted Prefixes & Minimum Swap Invariants\n\n## 1. Historical Context & Motivation\n\nIn early hardware architectures, memory writes were significantly slower than memory reads. Writing data to magnetic drums or early solid-state disks required waiting for mechanical rotations or physical charge accumulations, which caused performance bottlenecks. \n\nWhile algorithms like Bubble Sort or Insertion Sort write to memory frequently by swapping elements repeatedly, **Selection Sort** was designed to minimize memory write cycles.\n\nSelection Sort divides the array into a sorted prefix and an unsorted suffix. \n\nBy scanning the unsorted suffix to locate the minimum element and performing **only one swap per pass**, Selection Sort guarantees that the total number of swaps is bounded by $N-1$. This makes it highly efficient for legacy storage systems and flash memory (EEPROM) devices, where reducing write cycles extends the lifespan of the hardware. Today, Selection Sort is used in memory-limited embedded controllers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Mathematical Invariants\n\n### Prefix-Suffix Partitioning\nAt any step $i$ in Selection Sort:\n1.  **Sorted Prefix**: The subarray $A[0 \\dots i-1]$ is sorted in ascending order and contains the $i$ smallest elements of the entire array.\n2.  **Unsorted Suffix**: The subarray $A[i \\dots N-1]$ contains the remaining elements.\n\n---\n\n### Minimum Element Selection\nDuring each pass:\n*   We scan the unsorted suffix $A[i \\dots N-1]$ to find the index $min\\_idx$ of the smallest element:\n\n$$A[min\\_idx] = \\min(A[i \\dots N-1])$$\n\n*   We swap $A[i]$ with $A[min\\_idx]$, moving the minimum element to the end of the sorted prefix.\n*   This invariant guarantees that the swap count is at most $O(N)$, which is lower than Bubble Sort's worst-case swap count of $O(N^2)$.\n\n### Unsorted Suffix Minimum Selection\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 220\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"220\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#3b82f6\" font-size=\"14\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">Selection Sort Prefix-Suffix Swap</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(50, 60)\">\n    {/* Sorted Prefix */}\n    <rect x=\"0\" y=\"10\" width=\"65\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.15)\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"32\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#10b981\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Sorted: [1, 2]</text>\n\n    {/* Unsorted Suffix */}\n    <g transform=\"translate(75, 0)\">\n      <rect x=\"0\" y=\"10\" width=\"30\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <text x=\"15\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">8</text>\n\n      <rect x=\"40\" y=\"10\" width=\"30\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#1e1b4b\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n      <text x=\"55\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">3 (Min)</text>\n\n      <rect x=\"80\" y=\"10\" width=\"30\" height=\"30\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#71717a\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n      <text x=\"95\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">5</text>\n    </g>\n\n    {/* Swap Path */}\n    <path d=\"M 90,50 Q 110,70 130,50\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#eab308\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-select)\"/>\n    <text x=\"110\" y=\"70\" fill=\"#eab308\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Swap Min to Boundary</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-select\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#eab308\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Step-by-Step Execution Trace\n\nLet us trace sorting the array `A = [64, 25, 12, 22, 11]` using Selection Sort.\n\n### Trace Table\n\n| Pass ($i$) | Sorted Prefix Bounds | Unsorted Suffix | Min Element Index found | Min Value | Swap Targets | Resulting Array State |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **0** | Empty | `[64, 25, 12, 22, 11]` | $4$ | $11$ | Swap $A[0]$ and $A[4]$ | `[11, 25, 12, 22, 64]` |\n| **1** | `[11]` | `[25, 12, 22, 64]` | $2$ | $12$ | Swap $A[1]$ and $A[2]$ | `[11, 12, 25, 22, 64]` |\n| **2** | `[11, 12]` | `[25, 22, 64]` | $3$ | $22$ | Swap $A[2]$ and $A[3]$ | `[11, 12, 22, 25, 64]` |\n| **3** | `[11, 12, 22]` | `[25, 64]` | $3$ | $25$ | Swap $A[3]$ and $A[3]$ | `[11, 12, 22, 25, 64]` |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Production-Ready Implementations\n\n### C++ Source Code\n```cpp\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <iostream>\n\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass SelectionSort {\npublic:\n    void sort(vector<int>& arr) {\n        int n = arr.size();\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n            // Find the minimum element in unsorted array\n            int min_idx = i;\n            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n                if (arr[j] < arr[min_idx]) {\n                    min_idx = j;\n                }\n            }\n\n            // Swap the found minimum element with the first element\n            if (min_idx != i) {\n                swap(arr[i], arr[min_idx]);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n};\n```\n\n### Python Source Code\n```python\nfrom typing import List\n\nclass SelectionSort:\n    def sort(self, arr: List[int]):\n        n = len(arr)\n        for i in range(n - 1):\n            # Find the minimum element in unsorted array\n            min_idx = i\n            for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                if arr[j] < arr[min_idx]:\n                    min_idx = j\n            \n            # Swap the found minimum element with the first element\n            if min_idx != i:\n                arr[i], arr[min_idx] = arr[min_idx], arr[i]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Asymptotic Time & Space Complexity\n\n*   **Time Complexity**: Worse, Average, and Best case time complexity are all **$O(N^2)$**. Selection Sort must scan the entire unsorted suffix to locate the minimum element at each step, regardless of the initial array order.\n*   **Swap Complexity**: $O(N)$ swaps. At most $N-1$ swap operations are executed.\n*   **Space Complexity**: $O(1)$ auxiliary space (in-place sorting).\n*   **Stability**: **Unstable**. Long swaps can change the relative order of duplicate elements. For example, sorting `[4a, 4b, 1]` swaps `4a` with `1`, placing it after `4b` and violating stability.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Systems Connections: EEPROM Flash Page Writes\n\nIn hardware engineering:\n*   **Flash Memory (EEPROM) Optimizations**: Solid-state memory cells degrade after a certain number of write cycles (often ~100,000 writes). Selection Sort is preferred over Bubble Sort or Insertion Sort to sort data on these devices, as its $O(N)$ write complexity minimizes write cycles and extends hardware life.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Sort Colors (LeetCode 75)](https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-colors/): Tri-color sorting.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practice simple in-place array partitioning.\n* [Selection Sort Mechanics (GeeksforGeeks)](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/selection-sort/): Basic implementation.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Understand minimum index searches.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Sort an Array (LeetCode 912)](https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-an-array/): Sorting benchmarks.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Highlights when $O(N \\log N)$ sorting algorithms (like Merge Sort) are preferred over Selection Sort.\n* [Find Peak Element (LeetCode 162)](https://leetcode.com/problems/find-peak-element/): Index sweeps.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices finding local maximums inside discrete arrays.\n",
