    "slug": "dfs-traversal",
    "markdown": "# DFS Traversal: Depth-First Dives & Backtracking Call Traces\n\nWhile BFS expands radially, exploring all paths simultaneously consumes memory for broad queue frontiers. To explore paths fully to their terminals before attempting alternatives, we use **Depth-First Search (DFS)**.\n\nThis chapter explores **DFS Traversal**. We focus on recursion stack frames, backtracking invariants, cycle tracking, and topological sort.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Depth First Dives: The Call Stack Frame\n\nDFS dives along a path as deep as possible before backtracking:\n- It can be written recursively, leveraging the operating system's native **Function Call Stack**, or iteratively using an explicit user-allocated stack.\n- **Backtracking**: When a node has no unvisited neighbors, the function returns, popping the node from the call stack and resuming execution at the predecessor node.\n\n### DFS Backtracking Path\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <rect width=\"600\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#000\" stroke=\"#27272a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#f472b6\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-weight=\"bold\">DFS Dive & Backtrack Path</text>\n\n  <g transform=\"translate(45, 60)\">\n    {/* Root */}\n    <circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <text x=\"50\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Start</text>\n\n    {/* Child 1 */}\n    <path d=\"M 64,50 L 116,50\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"50\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"130\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">N1</text>\n\n    {/* Leaf (dead end) */}\n    <path d=\"M 144,50 L 196,50\" stroke=\"#f472b6\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n    <circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"50\" r=\"14\" fill=\"#111\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n    <text x=\"210\" y=\"53\" fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Leaf</text>\n\n    {/* Backtrack Arrow */}\n    <path d=\"M 196,38 Q 130,20 64,38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#eab308\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 1\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow-dfs-back)\"/>\n    <text x=\"130\" y=\"15\" fill=\"#eab308\" font-size=\"8\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"monospace\">Backtrack to alternative branches</text>\n  </g>\n\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"arrow-dfs-back\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"4\" markerHeight=\"4\" orient=\"auto\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"#eab308\" />\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n</svg>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Practice Checklist\n\n### Beginner Problems\n* [Binary Tree Inorder Traversal (LeetCode 94)](https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-inorder-traversal/): Basic DFS recursion.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Build tree traversal basics.\n* [Path Sum (LeetCode 112)](https://leetcode.com/problems/path-sum/): DFS node searches.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Practices tracking cumulative path calculations.\n\n### Intermediate Problems\n* [Number of Islands (LeetCode 200)](https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-islands/): Grid cell traversal.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Uses DFS to sink island node clusters.\n* [Course Schedule (LeetCode 207)](https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule/): Cycle check sorting.\n  * *Why this is useful:* Applies DFS to detect dependency cycle overlaps.\n",
